This is a alternative way to transmit data versiuing copper cables. 

Fibre Optics uses: 

- A thin glass / plastic core surrounded by protection
- The diameter of a human hair
- Transmit light over the cable
- Can cover larger distances and higher speeds
- Resistant to EMI and less prone to water ingress
- More consistent (normally) vs copper cable

## FIbre Optic Cable (Construction)

Connectors influence what hardware the cable can be used with and can limit or influence the transmission characteristics.

Cable choice is essential this directly influences distance speed and features.

FIbre diameter is expressed as X/Y. e.g. 9/125 is the diameter of the core, Y is the diameter of the cladding (both in micros)

Core - This is the physical medium which carries light between transmitter and reciever

Cladding - This is boundary surrounding the core to contain the light which allows data to move through the length of the core 

Buffer - This is a combination of internal coating & strengthen fibres designed to absorb shocks and provides additional strength for the core. This is surrounded by a cable jacket.  

## FIbre Optic Cable (Mode)

Singlemode:   

- Has a really small core its often 8/9 microns in size.
- Generally uses a yellow jacket. But not always.
- Due to it tiny core light generally follows a fairly single and straight path down the core.
- Little distoriton and bounce.
- Longest distance - highest bandwidth
- Not the fastest but the best combo for hgih speed and distance by far.

Multimode: 

- Has a much larger core
- An orange/Aqua jacket
- More light more data
- Much faster but has more distortion
- Good for shorter cable runs
- Cheaper LED optics
- Uses prefix OM

## Fibre Optic Transceivers:

- SFP Transceiver Module (SFP or MINI GBIC)
- Single form-factor pluggable
- Generates and sends or receives light TO/FROM fibre
- Multi-mode or single-mode
- Optimised for a a cable type
- 100BASE-LX, 10GBASE-LR, 100GBASE-LR4 (This is used by vendors to tell you what cables you are using)
